TEST 38472 -- ATMF - Master
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-21, tb470
DUT:    IE520 4-member VCStack, AMF node "IE520-stk", configured as AMF MASTER
Peer:   AR4050S-5G, AMF node "4050-5g", AMF member
AMF:    network-name "tb470"; AMF link = po1 (LACP aggregate, switchport atmf-link)

VERDICT: FAIL -- steps 1, 2, 3 and 5 PASS; STEP 4 (secure mode) FAILED.
         Recorded and continued per standing policy. See FINDING below.

STEP 1 -- "Setup an AMF network with the DUT as the AMF master"        PASS
  Applied: peer -> no atmf master ; DUT -> atmf master
  Result:
    DUT   : Network Name tb470 / Node Name IE520-stk / Role Master / Nodes 2
    Peer  : Network Name tb470 / Node Name 4050-5g   / Role Member / Nodes 2
  show atmf nodes on the DUT:
      Node        Device           ATMF        Parent      Node
      Name        Type             Master  SC  Domain      Depth
    * IE520-stk   AT-IE520-28GSX     Y     S   none          0
      4050-5g     AR4050S-5G         N     N   IE520-stk     1

STEP 2 -- "Check nodes leaving and joining does not affect the DUT"    PASS
  Method: shutdown / no shutdown po1 on the MEMBER, so the DUT is never the
  device being changed.
      before       : DUT nodes = 2, Role = Master
      member gone  : DUT nodes = 1, Role = Master      <-- DUT unaffected
      rejoined     : DUT nodes = 2, Role = Master
  The DUT held mastership throughout and the node count tracked the member
  leaving and rejoining exactly once each way.
  CAVEAT: with only one other node on this bench, "nodes leaving and joining"
  is exercised at n=1. This does not test master stability under several nodes
  churning at once.

STEP 3 -- "atmf working-set group all creates a working set with all nodes"  PASS
      IE520-stk# atmf working-set group all
      ===================
      4050-5g, IE520-stk:
      ===================
      Working set join
      tb470[2]#
  POSITIVE PROOF the working set is real, not just a prompt change -- one
  command issued inside it executed on BOTH nodes and each answered for itself:
      tb470[2]# show atmf | include Node Name
      ========
      4050-5g:        Node Name : 4050-5g
      ==========
      IE520-stk:      Node Name : IE520-stk
  The prompt "tb470[2]#" carries the network name and the member count (2).

STEP 4 -- "atmf secure-mode enable-all; all nodes should leave and rejoin"  FAIL

  Command syntax note: "atmf secure-mode enable-all" is a PRIVILEGED EXEC
  command on this build, not a configure-mode one. The case text does not say
  which mode. In configure mode only "atmf secure-mode" (local node) exists.

  The command itself behaved correctly:
      IE520-stk# atmf secure-mode enable-all
      Total number of nodes 2
      2 nodes support secure-mode
      Enable secure-mode across the ATMF network ? (y/n): y
      IMISH: Please wait while nodes leave and rejoin the network with the
             updated setting.

  Secure mode did enable on BOTH nodes, and certificates were issued:
      show atmf secure-mode certificate (on the master)
        Node Name    Signer       Expires        Status
        IE520-stk    IE520-stk    19 Mar 2027    Active
        4050-5g      IE520-stk    19 Mar 2027    Active
      show atmf authorization pending  ->  empty (nothing waiting to be approved)

  *** THE NODES LEFT BUT NEVER REJOINED. ***
      DUT   : Secure Mode Enabled, Current ATMF Nodes 1
      Peer  : Secure Mode Enabled, Current ATMF Nodes 1
      Both sides, symmetrically:
        po1  Downlink/Uplink  Up  OneWay  <peer named correctly>  Blocking

  Each node names the other correctly as the adjacent node -- so hellos are
  being heard -- yet the adjacency never progresses past OneWay and the port
  stays Blocking. This persisted for more than 10 minutes and SURVIVED a full
  link flap (shutdown / no shutdown po1 on the member).

STEP 5 -- "Disable secure mode"                                        PASS
  The literal command from the case text does exist in privileged EXEC:
      IE520-stk# no atmf secure-mode enable-all
      % Warning: All security certificates will be deleted.
      Disable secure-mode across the ATMF network ? (y/n):
  Because the network was already split, it was disabled per node instead
  ("no atmf secure-mode" in configure mode on each), which is the only path
  that reaches both nodes when they cannot see each other.

  Result, within one settle period:
      DUT   : Secure Mode Disabled, Nodes 2, po1 Downlink Up FULL Forwarding
      Peer  : Secure Mode Disabled, Nodes 2, po1 Uplink   Up FULL Forwarding

=========================== FINDING =======================================
With ATMF secure mode ENABLED, this two-node AMF network does not form.
With it DISABLED, the SAME link on the SAME config goes Full/Forwarding
immediately. That is a clean A/B on one link:

    secure mode OFF -> po1 Full / Forwarding, 2 nodes   (observed repeatedly)
    secure mode ON  -> po1 OneWay / Blocking, 1 node    (>10 min, survived a
                                                         link flap)

Not explained by authorization: certificates were Active for both nodes and
the pending-authorization queue was empty.

=========================== OPERATOR ERROR ================================
Recorded because it is in the transcripts and would otherwise be confusing.
While probing the syntax, "atmf secure-mode ?" was sent by a driver that
appends a carriage return. "<cr>" was a valid completion, so the trailing CR
EXECUTED "atmf secure-mode" in configure mode and enabled secure mode on the
DUT alone. That split the network (master secure, member not). It was detected
on the next command ("ATMF Secure-mode is already enabled"), backed out with
"no atmf secure-mode", and the network re-formed to 2 nodes before step 4 was
run properly. No result above depends on that state.
LESSON: never send "<command> ?" through a driver that appends CR.

